 in [42], machine learning approaches rely on previous observations, thus, if the driver behavior is unlike previous observations, this may degrade performance. Lastly, data-driven strategies generally require large quantities of data, whereas state estimation techniques require little to no data to develop. Multi-modal trajectory prediction methods have also been proposed [24], [36], [40], [44], [45], which are based on machine learning (ML). Their reported aim is to predict a probability distribution of possible trajectories. These methods predict multiple trajectories at each time step and obtain a probability associated with each predicted path. While this work focuses on mainly predicting trajectories of vehicles in highways, a number of related publications have reported their application for prediction of human trajectories [46], [47], [48], [49], [50] Road geometry and information has been utilized to help facilitate trajectory prediction in [7], [13], [14], [51], and [27]. In these publications, vehicle motion is represented in roadway coordinates. Other methods incorporate road information using map features fed into a neural network [33], [35], [36], [37]. In this paper, the IMM approach presented in [7] and [13] is extended to predict lane-changing trajectories by including lane-change models in the IMM’s model-set for maneuver
recognition and prediction. Models are selected from [11] and [16], and are used to track a vehicle as well as identify its lane-keeping and lane-changing maneuvers. The models are defined in curvi-linear coordinates and are used in an IMM context for trajectory prediction. In general, prediction techniques that use IMM estimation apply KF-based filtering [6], [7], [11], [13], [16], [17], [52], which are not sufficiently robust to modelling uncertainties when the uncertainties cannot be represented by Gaussian noise with known statistics. In this paper, a more recently developed state estimation strategy based on a technique known as the Generalized Variable Boundary Layer-Smooth Variable Structure Filter (GVBLSVSF) [53] is applied for trajectory prediction. An advantage of the GVBL-SVSF compared to the KF is that it is robust to modelling uncertainties and stable given bounded uncertainties [53], [54]. The combination of the IMM and GVBL-SVSF, called IMM-GVBL-SVSF, is utilized to perform trajectory prediction. The performances of both the IMM-KF and the IMM-GVBL-SVSF are then compared in terms of prediction accuracy and stability using synthetic and real measurements. Note that only highway scenarios are considered in this paper. The SVSF has been applied to a number of different applications including target-tracking [53], [55], [56], robot localization and mapping [56], [57], robot manipulators [58], fault detection [59], electric power grids [60], electric vehicles [61], satellite state estimation [62], and neural network training [63]. Thus, another contribution of this paper is extending the application of SVSF to trajectory prediction for autonomous driving. The contributions of this paper can be summarized as:
• extending a trajectory prediction algorithm in curvi-linear coordinates to also predict lane-changing maneuvers; • developing a robust trajectory prediction technique using the IMM-GVBL-SVSF and extending the application of SVSF filtering to trajectory prediction; and, • comparison of the IMM-GVBL-SVSF to the IMM-KF in terms of trajectory prediction performance
Section II provides the problem formulation. The proposed algorithm summary, synthetic measurement generation, and the curvi-linear coordinate system are described in Section III. Section IV explains the proposed approach in more detail. Section V presents the simulation and experimental results from public data-sets, and provides a comparative analysis between the IMM-KF and IMM-GVBL-SVSF. This section also compares the proposed approach to some state-of-theart machine learning strategies to get an idea of what the performance gap is between AI methods. Section VI concludes the paper.
II. PROBLEM FORMULATION
A vehicle is a dynamic system, systems can be mathematically modelled using state-space representations, which are commonly of the form below [64]. The state of the dynamical system evolves as:
xk+1 = Fk xk + 0k uk + Gk vk (1)
