where xk is the state vector, uk is the control vector, and vk represents zero-mean Gaussian process noise with co-variance Qk. The matrices Fk, 0k, and Gk are the state transition matrix, input gain matrix, and noise gain matrix, respectively. In general, in target-tracking and trajectory prediction, the control vector uk is unknown because the actuator inputs of remote vehicles are not accessible. However, there are some methods [16], [65], [66] that model the vehicle motion with an optimal controller, meaning that the motion is governed by an optimal feedback control law, containing a term with uk. The objective of the proposed algorithm in this paper is to perform two tasks, which are 1) to estimate the current state of motion of a vehicle given noisy measurements, also known as target-tracking, and 2) to predict its future trajectory. In target-tracking, measurements of a target are received from sensors such as radars, cameras, and/or LIDARs [67], [68]. Measurements of the target include quantities such as the target’s position and velocity. In target-tracking, typically there are clutter measurements as well, which are measurements that do not belong to the target of interest [69]. The objective of data association [69] is to determine which measurement originates from which target. In this paper it is assumed that this step is already complete. The measurement model is defined as:
zk = Hk xk + wk (2)
where zk is the measurement vector at the current time step k, Hk is the measurement matrix, and wk is measurement noise, which is zero-mean and Gaussian with co-variance Rk. The objective in target-tracking is to estimate the state vector at the current time step k, denoted as xˆk|k, given measurement zk. In contrast, in trajectory prediction, the objective is to estimate the future trajectory of a vehicle in a time horizon given the current state estimate xˆk|k. Let tp represent the prediction horizon and N p denote the number of samples within the horizon. The task is to estimate the future state vector of a vehicle at time steps k + 1, k + 2, and up to k + N p. The predictions can be mathematically represented as a sequence of state vectors:
{xˆk+1|k , xˆk+2|k , . . . ., xˆk+Np|k } (3)
In this sequence, each predicted state vector at each future time step consists of state variables such as the predicted position, velocity, and acceleration. Note the subscript j|k, according to [64], that indicates a filtered state if j = k and a predicted state if j > k. In terms of the probability distribution, many filters such as the KF assume that the current state vector xk can be represented by a Gaussian distribution with a mean of xˆk|k and a covariance of Pk|k. The sequence of predicted state vectors can also represent the mean of a multivariate Gaussian probability distribution at each future time step. If j > k, the distribution of the state prediction at time step j in the future would have a mean of xˆ j|k and an associated covariance of P j|k. In other words, equation (3) represents the mean predicted state sequence in the next N p time steps and this is outputted by the algorithms in this paper. The associated covariance at each time step can
be outputted by the state estimation techniques as explained later, however, this is not considered in this paper.
III. ALGORITHM OVERVIEW, MEASUREMENT
GENERATION, AND CURVI-LINEAR COORDINATE SYSTEM
The proposed trajectory prediction method is comprised of two stages, which are i) vehicle tracking and behavior probability estimation; and, ii) path prediction for a specified time horizon of tp seconds. A highway scene is assumed. The process is illustrated in Figure 1. In stage-(1), that pertains to target-tracking and behavior probability estimation, a measurement is received in Cartesian coordinates and is then converted to the Curvi-linear Coordinate System (CCS). The algorithm uses the CCS to perform tracking, maneuver identification, and prediction. An IMM estimator uses the converted measurement to calculate the vehicle’s state of motion as well as update the probabilities of each selected maneuver at the current time step. The estimation algorithm used is the IMM-GVBL-SVSF. The vehicle’s current states and maneuver probabilities are used to perform path prediction. In stage-(2), the target trajectory is predicted given its current state estimates and the maneuver probabilities from the previous stage. The trajectory is predicted tp seconds ahead by having the IMM-GVBL-SVSF predictor recursively project N p (prediction horizon) time steps into future. The predicted state vectors make up the outputted trajectory.
