B. Curvi-Linear Coordinate System
1) Description: Typically, target-tracking is performed in Cartesian coordinates. However, a disadvantage of Cartesian coordinates is that when the task is to perform maneuver recognition and trajectory prediction, the Cartesian plane gives no restrictions on the motion of the vehicle. In other words, it allows the car to move in any direction, which is not the case in a real driving condition. For example, if the road-way is to turn (in the future but within the prediction horizon), the conventional Cartesian coordinate models may predict the trajectory to be outside the road boundaries as road curvature information is not utilized. Whereas in the Curvi-linear Coordinate System (CCS) [11], [70], the roadway geometry model restricts the motion to be within the road boundaries [11]. As illustrated in Figure 2, the vehicle moves along the curve, and this curve is one axis of the CCS, called the s-axis. The s-axis is in turn used to provide the arc-length or distance along the road. The other perpendicular axis gives the lateral position, which is the n-axis. To construct the saxis, a roadway curve must be selected. This can include the center-line of a lane, a lane-line with road markings on them, or a road boundary. Further to the above, the s-axis represents the longitudinal position and the n-axis gives the lateral position. A position of a vehicle in the CCS can be expressed as [sk nk
]T . Intuitively, as a car moves along a road in a highway, the car can either
stay in its current lane or change lanes. If it stays, then the car will move along the direction of the road only. If it changes lanes, it will move in the lateral direction as well. As such, the CCS makes it easier to recognize lane-keeping and lanechanging behaviors. Hence, the motion is restricted to those two directions. Therefore, when using the CCS for prediction, the models defined in this system will predict paths according to the road boundaries.
2) Conversion Between Coordinate Systems: Let pxy = [xp yp
]T be the remote vehicle’s position in Cartesian coordinates at an arbitrary time index p as shown in Figure 2. Coordinates of points along the selected roadway line to form the s-axis are required in order to convert to the CCS. These can be obtained from lane-markings detected by cameras [71] or from high-definition (HD) map databases [72]. Further to the illustration of the CCS shown in Figure 2, locations of the points on the center-line are utilized to obtain the saxis by using cubic spline fitting as described in [73] and [74]. According to [72], one benefit of using Cubic splines to implement the roadway geometry is that only way-points are required to obtain a representative road geometry model with ease. However, a disadvantage is that the shape of the approximated roadway geometry model can be significantly changed if one control point is altered [72]. According to [72], the error in using cubic splines to represent road geometry is 17.5786m. The s-axis gives the distance travelled by the remote vehicle, denoted as sp and the other axis is the n-axis, which gives the closest lateral distance to the s-axis curve, represented as n p. The point on the s-axis curve that is the closest to the vehicle is denoted as cp. Note that in Figure 2, if the remote vehicle lies below the axis, the shortest lateral distance gets negated. More details on the conversion between the coordinate systems can be found in [73], [74], and [7]. Reference [70] provides information on how to obtain the velocity along the s-axis and n-axis.
IV. PROPOSED APPROACH
