A. Vehicle Tracking
The IMM-GVBL-SVSF is essentially a robust target-tracking strategy and involves estimating the states of a moving object using uncertain, indirect, and/or inaccurate measurements. The measurement received at each time step is converted to the CCS, then the IMM-GVBL-SVSF technique is used to estimate the current states and behavior probabilities using the converted measurement. Multiple model (MM) estimation techniques assume that the system follows one model at each time step. There are two types of MM techniques, static and dynamic [64]. With dynamic MM techniques, it is assumed that the system follows different operating regimes overtime, with each represented by a model from a finite set [64]. In this paper, a dynamic MM is used because cars change behavior according to their maneuver overtime and each maneuver can be represented using a different motion model. The IMM estimator is computationally efficient but is a sub-optimal implementation of the dynamic MM [64]. Integral to the proposed IMM strategy is the GVBL-SVSF estimation strategy as follows [53]. 1) GVBL-SVSF: State-space systems expressed in discrete-time commonly follow the structure below:
xk+1 = Fk xk + Gk uk + vk (9)
zk = Hk xk + wk (10)
vk ∼ N (0, Qk) (11)
wk ∼ N (0, Rk) (12)
where Fk is the state transition matrix, Gk is the input gain matrix, uk is the control vector, and vk is process noise. In the
measurement model, Hk is the measurement matrix and wk is measurement noise. The GVBL-SVSF is a derivative of the original formulation of the Smooth Variable Structure Filter (SVSF) reported in 2007 [54]. Many advances have been proposed since, including the co-variance formulations [75], [76], combination with the Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) [77], combination with the Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF) [77], formulations with an optimal smoothing boundary layer [53], [78], 2nd-order SVSF [79], as well as the square-root SVSF [80]. The SVSF has a predictor-corrector form similar to the KF, but with a different gain formulation, based on the sliding mode control concept [54]. The SVSF gain is a discontinuous corrective term that keeps the state estimate moving back and forth across the true state trajectory [54]. A region is formed around the true trajectory and the state estimate stays within it [54], known as the existence subspace. The level of modelling uncertainties affects the width of the existence subspace [54]. The properties of stability as well as robustness to modelling error and disturbances are validated in [54], given that there exists upper limits for the level of modelling uncertainties and disturbances. However, the SVSF’s discontinuous corrective action results in chattering and to resolve this, a smoothing boundary layer, denoted by ψ may be used [54]. The smoothing boundary layer results in a linear interpolation of the discontinuous corrective action within the neighborhood ψ of the switching hyperplane [54]. This concept is illustrated in Figure 3, where β is the width of the existence subspace. In Figure 3a), the smoothing boundary layer width is larger than the existence subspace width, and in this case, chattering is removed. Conversely, in 3b), ψ is smaller than β, which results in chattering. In the GVBL-SVSF, the width of the boundary layer is made time variant and is optimally changed to within limits in order to eliminate chattering [53]. A