The IMM is an interpretable model-based strategy. In terms of features, as inputs, IMM strategies take the states estimated from the previous time step and measurements to obtain as outputs the current estimated state and mode probabilities. Afterwards the current state estimates and mode probabilities are used to obtain the predicted trajectory. The IMM is an information fusion strategy in the sense that it combines multiple sources of information, which would be the outputted states from each mode-matched filter. It computes a weighted sum of the outputted states using the mode probabilities as weights. For tracking and predicting trajectories of maneuvering targets, the IMM can be more beneficial than single model filters (e.g. KF) because for vehicles typically one model is not enough to represent multiple maneuvers. The features of the estimated states as well as measurements are useful for prediction because these features are used to identify maneuvers using mode probabilities. These features are used to compute the likelihood function 3 j
