To compare the strategies qualitatively, two specific vehicles from the HighD data-set are selected and are analyzed in terms of trajectory prediction. In Figures 14 and 15, the entire real trajectory of the vehicle, the predicted trajectories outputted by the IMM algorithms, as well as the ground truth predicted path are shown. Note that the ground truth predicted path refers to the real trajectory in the next N p time steps from the current
time step k. In these figures, the vertical axis gives the lateral position and the horizontal axis gives the longitudinal position. To begin, first comparisons are made for a lane-changing maneuver in Figure 14. In Figure 14a), this is when the time elapsed since the car appeared in the video is 4.48s and the predicted trajectories are shown in this figure. Note that 4.48s corresponds to roughly 2.48s into the lane-change maneuver because the lane-change begins at around 2s as shown in Figure 16, which is the lateral position of the vehicle overtime. In this case, the IMM-GVBL-SVSF predicts the trajectory slightly more accurately due to the SVSF’s properties of robustness to modelling error and stability. Next, in Figures 14b) and 14c), the predicted trajectories at 2.92s and 3.4s into the maneuver are shown, respectively. The prediction performance is comparable in both cases likely because the state estimates of both algorithms have converged at nearly the same points. As explained in section V-B, under the presence of modelling error, in theory, the state estimates of both IMM-KF and IMM-GVBL-SVSF can converge to nearly the same point as illustrated in Figure 7a) near the end of the turning simulation at about 37.5s. But its just that when modelling uncertainties are present, the state estimates of IMM-GVBL-SVSF would converge faster than IMM-KF since the KF is less stable than the SVSF. Moreover, for the lane-keeping case shown in Figure 15, the predicted trajectories are shown at 1.20s, 2.6s, and 4.24s into the maneuver. At these times the predicted trajectories are comparable from both IMM algorithms likely because the state estimates from both methods have converged to nearly the same point at those times, resulting in comparable state predictions. As stated above, the state estimates of both approaches can converge to nearly the same point, but under the presence of modelling uncertainty, the IMM-GVBL-SVSF would converge faster.
