position. Note that the reason why from 3.12s and beyond the probability stays above 0.8 in that figure is because the VT-LC model of the adjacent upper lane represents lane-converging motion to the upper lane’s center-line. This is expected because the car stays in the adjacent upper lane after the lane-change, thus, it follows the center-line of the upper-lane. Moreover, the variance of the measurement noise affects the maneuver detection and results in a maneuver detection latency. As discussed in the previous sub-section, the trajectories predicted while the car is in the delay period would be inaccurate and adversely affects the prediction performance of IMM-based strategies. As stated earlier, the prediction accuracy is partly dependent on whether the recognized maneuver is correct, thus, identifying the correct maneuver as soon as possible is essential. Moreover, the maneuver detection latency and delay is proportional to the magnitude of the noise variance. Therefore, on NGSIM data, due to larger noise levels, the detection delay to identify a lane-change would be greater than 1.12s. Thus, with a high noise variance, there would be greater delays leading to less accurate trajectory predictions. Here is the opportunity for augmenting IMM strategies with AI-based strategies to reduce maneuver detection latency.
VI. CONCLUSION
In this paper, a new method for trajectory prediction based on robust multiple model state estimation is proposed that accounts for the road geometry using the curvi-linear coordinate system (CCS). This technique is referred to as the Interacting Multiple Model - Generalized Variable Boundary
